==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|abaxile}}

===Etymology===
From {{affix|en|ab-|t1=away from|axial|t2=axis}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌæˈbæk.si.əl/|a=GenAm}}<ref>{{R:MW3 1976|page=2}}</ref> or {{IPA|en|/æbˈæk.si.əl/}}<ref>{{R:American Heritage Dictionary|edition=1971|page=2}}</ref>
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-abaxial.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{audio|en|en-ca-abaxial.ogg|a=CA}}
* {{hyphenation|en|ab|ax|i|al|caption=Hyphenation US}}, {{hyphenation|en|ab|ax|ial|caption=UK}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{lb|en|botany|zoology}} Of a side that is [[face|facing]] [[away]] from the [[axis]] or central line, such as the [[underside]] of a leaf, or the back of an animal. {{defdate|Mid 19<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref>{{R:SOED5|page=2}}</ref>
#* {{RQ:Schuster Hepaticae|volume=V|page=7|text=The lobule margins, furthermore, are arched away from the lobe, with the consequence that (when fully inflated) the '''abaxial''' leaf surface forms the interior lining of the lobule.}}
# {{lb|en|botany}} Not in the axis. Applied to an [[embryo]] placed out of the axis of the seed.<ref name=Jackson>{{cite book |last=Jackson |first=Benjamin Daydon |year=1928 |title=A glossary of botanic terms with their derivation and accent |url=https://archive.org/details/glossaryofbotani1928jack/page/1/mode/1up |location=Philadelphia |publisher=J. B. Lippincott Company |page=1}}</ref>

